window.docContents[144].resolve({"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___5___-Orthogonality-of-characters/Characters-defined-without-representations-via-convolution-algebra/#Chapter4___Remark4___5___3":{"contents":"Remark 4.5.3. We see that characters of irreducible complex representations of G can be defined without mentioning irreducible representations. Namely, equip the space F(G, \\mathbb{C}) of complex-valued functions on G with the convolution product\n\n(f * g)(z) = \\sum_{x, y \\in G : xy = z} f(x)g(y).\n\nThis product turns F(G, \\mathbb{C}) into an associative algebra, with unit \\delta_e (the characteristic function of the unit e \\in G), and the space of class functions F_c(G, \\mathbb{C}) is a commutative subalgebra. Then one can define renormalized characters \\tilde{\\chi}_i \\in F_c(G, \\mathbb{C}) to be the primitive idempotents in this algebra, i.e., solutions of the equation f * f = f which cannot be decomposed into a sum of other nonzero solutions. Then one can define the characters by the formula\n\n\\chi_i(g) = \\sqrt{\\frac{|G|}{\\tilde{\\chi}_i(1)}} \\tilde{\\chi}_i(g)\n\n(check it!). This is, essentially, how Frobenius defined characters (see [Cu], equation (7)). Note that Frobenius defined representations at approximately the same time, but for some time it was not clear that there is a simple relation between irreducible representations and characters (namely, that irreducible characters are simply traces of group elements in irreducible representations). Even today, many group theorists sometimes talk of irreducible characters of a finite group rather than irreducible representations.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t4. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\t4.5. Orthogonality of characters\tCharacters defined without representations via convolution algebra","header":"Characters defined without representations via convolution algebra","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___5___-Orthogonality-of-characters/Characters-defined-without-representations-via-convolution-algebra/#Chapter4___Remark4___5___3"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___5___-Orthogonality-of-characters/Section-4___5___-Orthogonality-of-characters-___-Hermitian-inner-product-on-class-functions/#Chapter4___Introduction_4___5":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t4. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\t4.5. Orthogonality of characters\tSection 4.5: Orthogonality of characters — Hermitian inner product on class functions","header":"Section 4.5: Orthogonality of characters — Hermitian inner product on class functions","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___5___-Orthogonality-of-characters/Section-4___5___-Orthogonality-of-characters-___-Hermitian-inner-product-on-class-functions/#Chapter4___Introduction_4___5"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___12___-The-tensor-algebra/Section-2___12___-The-tensor-algebra-___-heading-and-introduction/#Chapter2___Discussion_2___12_heading":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t2. Basic notions of representation theory\t2.12. The tensor algebra\tSection 2.12: The tensor algebra — heading and introduction","header":"Section 2.12: The tensor algebra — heading and introduction","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___12___-The-tensor-algebra/Section-2___12___-The-tensor-algebra-___-heading-and-introduction/#Chapter2___Discussion_2___12_heading"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___15___-The-Frobenius-character-formula/Frobenius-character-formula-for-Specht-modules-V_lambda/#Chapter5___Theorem5___15___1":{"contents":"Theorem 5.15.1. Let N \\geq p. Then \\chi_{V_\\lambda}(C_\\mathbf{i}) is the coefficient of x^{\\lambda+\\rho} := \\prod x_j^{\\lambda_j + N - j} in the polynomial\n\n\\Delta(x) \\prod_{m \\geq 1} H_m(x)^{i_m}.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t5. Representations of finite groups: Further results\t5.15. The Frobenius character formula\tFrobenius character formula for Specht modules V_lambda","header":"Frobenius character formula for Specht modules V_lambda","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___15___-The-Frobenius-character-formula/Frobenius-character-formula-for-Specht-modules-V_lambda/#Chapter5___Theorem5___15___1"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___20___-Historical-interlude___-Hermann-Weyl-at-the-intersection-of-limitation-and-freedom/Section-5___20___-Historical-interlude-___-Hermann-Weyl/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___20___heading-1":{"contents":"","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t5. Representations of finite groups: Further results\t5.20. Historical interlude: Hermann Weyl at the intersection of limitation and freedom\tSection 5.20: Historical interlude — Hermann Weyl","header":"5.20. Historical interlude: Hermann Weyl at the intersection of limitation and freedom","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___20___-Historical-interlude___-Hermann-Weyl-at-the-intersection-of-limitation-and-freedom/Section-5___20___-Historical-interlude-___-Hermann-Weyl/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___20___heading-1"}});
